#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217742 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aac5cb30-5659-547e-bedf-921f6c99067a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "868dc18d-0fad-5252-9e03-483cfc1d7c47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 4cx + c^{2} - c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216544} {:answer/id #uuid "f079dec4-3142-58e5-90c0-9a16f6ef2900" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = c^{2}x + 4c^{2} - c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216545} {:answer/id #uuid "073d6a9d-198e-5784-b3e5-1dd0c9bd570e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = cx + 4c^{2} - c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216546} {:answer/id #uuid "135559d1-290a-5809-97ef-aa7fccb74af2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = cx + 8c^{2} - c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216547} {:answer/id #uuid "5f2f20df-4333-5c08-8d54-bf18ad3e7ff2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = cx + c^{2} - c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216548}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "073d6a9d-198e-5784-b3e5-1dd0c9bd570e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = cx + 4c^{2} - c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216546} :answer-field/id #uuid "6161c43b-09f8-5840-9540-3f9f42074fb4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216543}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "02a1418b-4b58-5661-becc-d4addff055b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-334202" :question/problem "Find the general solution of the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+4({y}^{′})^{2}-{y}^{′}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+4({y}^{′})^{2}-{y}^{′},\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp+4{p}^{2}-p.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp+4{p}^{2}-p)^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}+8p{p}^{′}-{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x+8p-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, which means at least one of the factors must be zero.\n\nThe general solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if ${p}^{′}=0.$ Then, $p$ is constant, say \n\n$$\np=c.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $p=c$ into $y=xp+4{p}^{2}-p,$ we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny=cx+4{c}^{2}-c.\n$$\n\nThis represents a family of straight lines, one line for each constant value of $c.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382595 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aac5cb30-5659-547e-bedf-921f6c99067a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4327a05-2572-52ce-b7a3-1c7e8e4e8354" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={c}^{2}x+{c}^{3}-2c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382597} {:answer/id #uuid "1258649e-a167-5438-a5ce-53173eb41888" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={c}^{3}x+{c}^{3}-2c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382598} {:answer/id #uuid "f0b66cc4-7f78-5e90-bcfd-5f782b836a8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=cx+{c}^{3}-2c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382599} {:answer/id #uuid "654a8f27-f4f5-5530-a010-d1511f035c33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=cx+2{c}^{3}-2c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382600} {:answer/id #uuid "019b0c8a-6b1b-520f-aff7-84c20fe95c36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=cx+{c}^{2}-2c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382601}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0b66cc4-7f78-5e90-bcfd-5f782b836a8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=cx+{c}^{3}-2c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382599} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d67aab9-73af-5fc9-afac-f2cb1374dbea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382596}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "284f78d5-711d-538b-9fce-2df62ab28f45" :question/math-academy-id "q-334203" :question/problem "Find the general solution of the Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+({y}^{′})^{3}-2{y}^{′}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+({y}^{′})^{3}-2{y}^{′},\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp+{p}^{3}-2p.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp+{p}^{3}-2p)^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}+3{p}^{2}{p}^{′}-2{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x+3{p}^{2}-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, which means at least one of the factors must be zero.\n\nThe general solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if ${p}^{′}=0.$ Then, $p$ is constant, say \n\n$$\np=c.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $p=c$ into $y=xp+{p}^{3}-2p,$ we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny=cx+{c}^{3}-2c.\n$$\n\nThis represents a family of straight lines, one line for each constant value of $c.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382602 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aac5cb30-5659-547e-bedf-921f6c99067a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b13115f3-906f-5193-99bb-1a1901e0d883" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=cx+c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382604} {:answer/id #uuid "89034d07-4829-5254-a3b9-249d0be9bea8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=cx+\\frac{1}{2c}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382605} {:answer/id #uuid "9b625242-479c-5943-9a7f-a03b4d67a566" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={c}^{2}x+\\frac{1}{c}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382606} {:answer/id #uuid "9ea004f6-60a3-513e-83dc-66736662fe04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=cx+\\frac{1}{c}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382607} {:answer/id #uuid "a0fc36dd-a482-549c-8fbc-83098f16eb1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={c}^{3}x+\\frac{1}{c}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382608}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ea004f6-60a3-513e-83dc-66736662fe04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=cx+\\frac{1}{c}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382607} :answer-field/id #uuid "7efd41de-7ae6-5e88-8a98-2eda4a55bc39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382603}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "796e83ce-3077-5464-bc5e-9be0563f9d98" :question/math-academy-id "q-334210" :question/problem "Find the general solution of the Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}},\\,{y}^{′}\\ne 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}},\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp+\\frac{1}{p}.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={(xp+\\frac{1}{p})}^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}-\\frac{1}{{p}^{2}}{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x-\\frac{1}{{p}^{2}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, which means at least one of the factors must be zero.\n\nThe general solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if ${p}^{′}=0.$ Then, $p$ is constant, say \n\n$$\np=c,\n$$\n\nwith the restriction $c\\ne 0.$\n\nSubstituting $p=c$ into $y=xp+\\frac{1}{p},$ we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny=cx+\\frac{1}{c}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a family of straight lines, one line for each constant value of $c\\ne 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382609 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ddcff62-d052-51fd-9e2b-50358fa02de5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Singular Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc15b468-9835-5cc4-9215-8542fefe13dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{4x},\\,x<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382611} {:answer/id #uuid "ce158720-1fc7-527b-87d0-908f5cce6c4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\\,x<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382612} {:answer/id #uuid "922361bf-f167-5db2-a557-55ff20814d20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{x},\\,x<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382613} {:answer/id #uuid "d08d2f5c-20e6-5fc4-8cdb-ff66476287ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2x},\\,x<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382614} {:answer/id #uuid "5aa110e4-0af4-5d43-9f6d-7aeda212de3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{x},\\,x>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382615}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc15b468-9835-5cc4-9215-8542fefe13dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{4x},\\,x<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382611} :answer-field/id #uuid "d95d9587-c73a-57a9-9174-9e73c054a9e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382610}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "19fc08b5-a70b-5aaf-9e22-7a42cd64e91f" :question/math-academy-id "q-334453" :question/problem "Find the singular solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+\\sqrt{{y}^{′}},\\,{y}^{′}\\ge 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nTo solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+\\sqrt{{y}^{′}},\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp+\\sqrt{p}.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp+\\sqrt{p})^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{p}}{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{p}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, so at least one factor must be zero.\n\nThe singular solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if $x+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{p}}=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{p}}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute this into $y=xp+\\sqrt{p}\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =xp+\\sqrt{p} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{p}})p+\\sqrt{p} \\\\  & =-\\frac{p}{2\\sqrt{p}}+\\sqrt{p} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{p}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the singular solution is given parametrically by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{p}} \\\\ y=\\frac{\\sqrt{p}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo eliminate $p,$ we first solve the first equation for $\\sqrt{p}\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{p}=-\\frac{1}{2x}\n$$\n\nSince $x=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{p}},$ we must have $x<0.$\n\nNext, let's substitute this expression for $\\sqrt{p}$ into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{\\sqrt{p}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{2x})}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the explicit form of the singular solution is\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{4x},\\,x<0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382616 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ddcff62-d052-51fd-9e2b-50358fa02de5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Singular Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "424bbb5c-ce45-5484-ae8a-0478fc3620bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)),\\,-1\\le x\\le 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382618} {:answer/id #uuid "0030d570-af4b-5827-8618-b37e058d369b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)),\\,-1\\le x\\le 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382619} {:answer/id #uuid "ca74d12f-cf40-58d9-b177-25f3b72dc052" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)),\\,-1\\le x\\le 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382620} {:answer/id #uuid "e48d63ef-c40b-5f1d-ab43-32f57ab8213d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)),\\,-1\\le x\\le 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382621} {:answer/id #uuid "fd027dd3-c6d7-5695-aa9b-fab52309c8d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)),\\,-1\\le x\\le 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382622}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e48d63ef-c40b-5f1d-ab43-32f57ab8213d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)),\\,-1\\le x\\le 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382621} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b84fb95-2319-5db5-bf51-b8e12537dc88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382617}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "433e97c3-88c2-5dfc-9c81-2ab81a0cbb54" :question/math-academy-id "q-334457" :question/problem "Find the singular solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{′}),\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le {y}^{′}\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nTo solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(p).\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(p))^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(p){p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(p))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, so at least one factor must be zero.\n\nThe singular solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if $x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(p)=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(p).\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute this into $y=xp+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(p):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =xp+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(p) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(p))p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(p) \\\\  & =p\\operatorname{sin}⁡(p)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(p)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the singular solution is given parametrically by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(p) \\\\ y=p\\operatorname{sin}⁡(p)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(p)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo eliminate $p,$ we first solve the first equation for $p:$\n\n$$\np=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)\n$$\n\nThis is valid only for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le p\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNext, let's substitute this expression for $p$ into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =p\\operatorname{sin}⁡(p)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(p) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)\\cdot x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the explicit form of the singular solution is\n\n$$\ny=x\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)),\\,-1\\le x\\le 1.\n$$"}]}
